      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chapinero"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The locality’s name comes from 16th‑century shoemakers who crafted chapines, sturdy shoes for walking through Bogotá’s muddy terrain.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://visitbogota.co/en/chapinero"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Chapinero is the heart of Bogotá’s gay community, hosting more than 100 bars, discos, and clubs that cater to the scene.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chapinero"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Historically, Chapinero sat on the main thoroughfare that linked Bogotá to the northern parts of the country.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chapinero"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Mule‑pulled trams reached Chapinero in 1875, and electric tracks followed in 1910, marking early public transit development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chapinero"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "To the east, Chapinero borders the Eastern Hills, giving the neighborhood a distinctive natural backdrop.",
